\begin_layout Section
ABSTRACT FOR SUBMISSION (first 2 paragraphs above with a few less URL's)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Mega2 program has been facilitating data-reformatting for linkage and
 analysis programs since 2000.
 Additional analysis programs have been added over time, though this has
 required software developers keenly knowledgeable in the Mega2 internals.
 Currently, Mega2 converts data from several different genetic analysis
 data formats (including PLINK, VCF, BCF, IMPUTE2) into the specific data
 requirements for over 40 commonly used linkage and association analysis
 programs (including Mendel, Merlin, Morgan, SHAPEIT, ROADTRIPS, MaCH/minimac3,
 etc.).
 Recently, Mega2 has been enhanced to use an SQLite database as an intermediate
 data representation.
 Additionally, bialleleic genotype data are now stored in a highly compressed
 form, much like that of the GenABEL R facility and the PLINK binary format.
 Meanwhile, the R community and Bioconductor community have developed a
 variety of genetic analysis programs complimentary to the programs available
 through Mega2.
 We have made it possible to load SQLite3 Mega2 databases easily and directly
 into R.
 After loading, accessing data frames holding these data for any purpose
 is straightforward in R.
 In addition, we have made C++ functions for R to decompress subsets of
 the genotype data that are available in the Mega2 database.
 The extra memory is only temporarily necessary; the data are returned as
 a simple matrix.
 We created the base Mega2R package that loads and manipulates data frames.
 We have also created several more R packages that illustrate how to use
 the data frames as well as perform useful functions: one to run the Bioconducto
r pedgene package to carry out gene-based association tests on family data
 using selected marker subsets, one to output the Mega2R data frames data
 as a VCF file and related files (for phenotype and family data), and one
 to convert the data frames into GenABEL R gwaa.data-class objects.
 The Mega2R program can be used to enhance GenABEL since it supports additional
 input data formats (such as PLINK, VCF and IMPUTE2); thus Mega2R allows
 these formats to be easily processed by GenABEL.
 Mega2 is open source and is freely available, along with extensive documentatio
n and a 'quick-start' tutorial, from our https://watson.hgen.pitt.edu/register
 web site.
 This work was supported by NIH grant R01 GM076667 (PI: Weeks).
 Earlier contributions to our initial code base were made by Nandita Mukhopadhya
y, Lee Almasy, Mark Schroeder, and William P.
 Mulvihill.
\end_layout

