\begin_layout Subsection
Running 
\change_deleted 1332494340 1496766438
CRAN
\change_unchanged
 pedgene
\change_inserted 1332494340 1496766635
 using the mega2pedgene R package
\change_unchanged

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
mega2pedgene
\emph default
 is the exception that proves the rule.
 Rather than use the 
\emph on
read.Mega2DB
\emph default
 function described above and as used by all the other packages, the start
 function is 
\emph on
init_pedgene
\emph default
.
 This function calls a utility function created for 
\emph on
read.Mega2DB
\emph default
, then it created, edits, and rewrites the 
\series bold
fam
\series default
 data frame to purge persons without known case/control status.
 It also calculates and stores some values that it will use repeatedly.
 viz:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
library(mega2pedgene)
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
ENV = init_pedgene(
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

srqsimr.db
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, verbose = T)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
mega2R
\series default
 has an internal default list of the chromosome and base pair ranges for
 a number of gene transcripts.
 The function 
\emph on
run_pedgene
\emph default
 examines the first 100 transcripts and prints the results.
 However, our test data is from part of the first chromosome only; our first
 100 probes find no data to run 
\emph on
pedgene
\emph default
 on.
 You may change the transcript list as explained in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:mega2-Package-Marker"

\end_inset

.
 It would be better to run 
\emph on
pedgene
\emph default
 on all the transcript entries.
 So type:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
applyFnToRanges(DOpedgene, ENV$refRanges, ENV$refIndices, envir = ENV)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You will see many reports of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

No markers in range
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, but occasionally you will see a listing of markers and a gene name.
 The genotypes for these markers, along with the markers info are fed to
 the call back function 
\emph on
DOpedgene
\emph default
.
 
\emph on
DOpedgene
\emph default
 converts the genotype nucleotides to the count of the minor allele and
 then it runs 
\emph on
pedgene
\emph default
 for several different marker weights.
 The results are automatically stored in a file, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

k_Schaid_rare.txt
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 Lines in that file have a prefix of chromosome, gene, number of markers
 and base pair range followed by 6 p-values.
 Two for each of three weightings of the the markers.
 You will see that gene FAM5C and TRABD2B are the only ones that have any
 p-values less than .005.
 If we knew this fact to start with, and hence these were the only two genes
 we were interested in, we could have typed:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
applyFnToGenes(DOpedgene, genes_arg = c("FAM5C", "TRABD2B"), envir = ENV)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(NOTE: FAM5C is an alias for BRINP3.) This function does not use the internal
 transcript list but uses Bioconductor information about the transcripts.
 This database is further explained in section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:mega2-Package-Marker"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Writing a VCF File
\change_inserted 1332494340 1496766624
 using the mega2vcf R package
\change_unchanged

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Creating the VCF file and related files is accomplished by typing:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
dir.create(
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

vcfr
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
library(mega2vcf)
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Mega2VCF(
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

vcfr/vcf
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This first creates the directory 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

vcfr
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

; so we can keep all the generated data in one place.
 Mega2VCF takes an argument which is the prefix used on all the files it
 generates.
 We have chosen to make that a path include a directory.
 Mega2VCF is explained in greater depth in the Implementation Section [
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:mega2vcf-Package-Functions"

\end_inset

].
 The Mega2VCF function assumes the environment is contained in the global
 variable ENV.
 If this is not true supply an additional argument 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

envir
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 set to the environment you wish to use.
 Note, that Mega2VCF will create a traditional .vcf file as well as a .fam
 file (we do linkage), a .phe file (phenotypes) and a few others.
 These files are explained further in the implementation section.
 Also read the code to see how the file data is packaged from the data frames.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Creating a GenABEL gwaa.data-class object
\change_inserted 1332494340 1496766661
 using the mega2genabel R package
\change_unchanged

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This task is analogous to the previous exercise.
 Type:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
library(mega2genabel)
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
seqsimgwaa = Mega2GenAbel(
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

MEGA
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The argument to Mega2GenAbel is a filename prefix that will be used to name
 the .tped/.fam files that are created as an intermediate to be read by GenABEL
 to producte a GenABEL 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

raw
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 file.
 Then the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

raw
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 file and phenotype file are processed to yield a gwaa.data-class object.
 (See the Implementation Section [
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:mega2genabel-Package-Functions"

\end_inset

].) You can use any of the GenABEL provided functions on your data, seqsimgwaa.
 Note: It would not be efficient to take the simple PLINK seqsimr data on
 this path (convert via Mega2 to a SQLite database then to .tped/.fam and
 then to get it into GenABEL).
 But there are several data formats that Mega2 can read and transform into
 a database that are not directly acceptable to GenABEL.
 So any Mega2 database that can be read into R,can be transformed to GenABEL
 analogous to this example with seqsimr.db.
\end_layout

