\begin_layout Subsubsection

\emph on
read.Mega2DB
\emph default
 function and tables
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\emph on
mega2
\emph default
 package function, 
\emph on
read.Mega2DB
\emph default
, reads the necessary tables from an SQLite database into a collection of
 data frames.
 It returns an 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

R environment
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 containing these data.
 If the verbose flag is true, summary statistics will be printed as the
 data frames are created.
 Variables ending in 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\series bold
_table
\series default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, viz.
 person_table, locus_table, are raw data directly copied from the SQLite
 database, although some of the original fields are only necessary for C++
 Mega2 and not copied.
 Other data frames in the R environment are derived information.
 The 
\series bold
fam
\series default
 data frame collects data from the 
\series bold
pedigree_table, person_table,
\emph on
 
\series default
\emph default
and 
\series bold
phenotype_table
\series default
 to produce the data frame equivalent of a PLINK .fam file.
 The 
\series bold
unified_genotype_table
\series default
 collects all the raw data bytes by chromosome from the 
\series bold
genotype_table
\series default
 for each person and makes a single new vector for each person.
 But the 
\series bold
unified_genotype_table 
\series default
can introduce up to three 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

virtual markers
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 at the end of each chromosome if the number of chromosomes is not an exact
 multiple of 4 (genotypes per byte).
 The 
\series bold
marker_table
\series default
 contains the offset of each marker in the genotype data vector assuming
 all the markers across all the chromosomes are contiguous.
 We construct an analogous offset for the 
\series bold
unified_genotype_table
\series default
 and add it to the 
\series bold
marker_table
\series default
.
 The 
\series bold
marker
\series default
\emph on
 
\emph default
data frame is a subset of the 
\series bold
marker_table
\series default
 data containing the name, chromosome, position, original marker offset
 and marker offset for the 
\series bold
unified_genotype_table
\series default
.
 (Note: Creating the 
\series bold
unified_genotype_table
\series default
 in compressed form with no 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

virtual markers
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 would require a lot of bit manipulations in R which would not be terribly
 efficient while keeping track of the added gaps is not particularly hard.
 We could use Rcpp but why use it if there are other alternatives.
 Of course, the Mega2 C++ program is written C++ and so does, in fact, just
 copy the bits it needs.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\emph on
mkfam 
\emph default
and
\emph on
 setfam
\emph default
 functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\emph on
mega2
\emph default
 package function, 
\emph on
setfam
\emph default
, may be not needed.
 The function, 
\emph on
mkfam
\emph default
, was called by default from 
\emph on
read.Mega2DB
\emph default
 to create the 
\series bold
fam
\series default
 table.
 It pulls together the rows from the 
\series bold
pedigree_table, person_table,
\emph on
 
\series default
\emph default
and 
\series bold
phenotype_table
\series default
 to make a 
\series bold
fam
\series default
 data frame.
 The 
\series bold
pedigree_table
\series default
 has a pedigree_link
\series bold
, person_table
\series default
 has a pedigree_link and person_link
\series bold
,
\emph on
 
\series default
\emph default
and 
\series bold
phenotype_table
\series default
 has a person_link field.
 These link fields are used by the R merge function operations to assemble
 data.
 As you might imagine, the 
\series bold
genotype_table
\series default
 also has a person_link field.
 (Note: The analog to these person 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

_link
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 fields for marker data frames is the locus_link field.) You may want to
 prune the 
\series bold
fam
\series default
 data frame to restrict the samples processed.
 For, example you might want to eliminate samples with unavailable case/control
 status.
 After you prune the table, 
\emph on
setfam
\emph default
 will set the 
\series bold
fam
\series default
 data frame in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

R environment
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 It will also prune the 
\series bold
phenotype_table
\series default
 and 
\series bold
genotype_table
\series default
 in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

R environment
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 to contain only those persons left in the 
\series bold
fam
\series default
 data frame.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*

\emph on
getgenotypes
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
getgenotypesraw
\emph default
 functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now we get to the good stuff; its time to extract genotype data.
 We presume that we have adjusted the 
\series bold
fam
\series default
 table as necessary.
 The functions, 
\emph on
getgenotypes
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
getgenotypesraw
\emph default
 return a matrix of nucleotide pairs or encode integers with a column for
 each marker containing a row per sample.
 Both functions take a single argument which is a subset of the 
\series bold
marker
\series default
 data frame.
 The
\series bold
 marker 
\series default
data frame has two offsets that are used internally by the decompression
 code; the 
\series bold
marker
\series default
 also has a name, chromosome and position that can be used to identify the
 data to the user.
 The two 
\emph on
getgenotypes
\emph default
 functions are really dispatches that call into Rcpp code.
 They call the proper function for the level of compression: biallelic and
 more than two alleles.
 They also collect genotypes and alleles data from appropriate 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

R environment
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 data frames to pass as explicit arguments to the Rcpp code.
 For each marker specified, 
\emph on
getgenotypes
\emph default
 will return the corresponding pair of nucleotides as a string.
 
\emph on
getgenotypesraw
\emph default
 will combine the first allele integer value and the second allele integer
 value; the two integers are returned as a single value with the integer
 for the first allele shifted 16 bits and or'ed to the integer for the second
 allele.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection

\emph on
mega2 
\emph default
Package Marker Range Functions
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:mega2-Package-Marker"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We might have compressed genotype data to create a 1000's of samples by
 1,0000,000's of markers matrix in R.
 But are unlikely to have the memory to support this large a matrix.
 Our genotype data is compressed, but we probably do not need to decompress
 it all at once.
 We introduce functions to partition the set of markers into reasonable
 chunks.
 For example, we may only want to process a chromosome or only just a few
 genes at a time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\emph on
setGenes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If we can not readily process all the markers at once, what do we typically
 want to do is look at some genes.
 Presumably, we want to get all the genotypes associated with the markers
 of some collection of genes.
 We have imported the Bioconductor data Annotations: "org.Hs.eg.db"and "TxDb.Hsapien
s.UCSC.hg19.knownGene" into the 
\emph on
mega2
\emph default
 package.
 The former translates between a gene name or alias and the entrez ID of
 the gene.
 The latter gives start position, end position and chromosome for each transcrip
t known for the each gene.
 You might want to use the tanscript data from another build available at
 Bioconductor or follow their instructions make your own mapping.
 The 
\emph on
mega2
\emph default
 function 
\emph on
setGenes
\emph default
 lets you supply alternate string names for these data.
 Mega2 will load the corresponding Namespace from R when you access the
 
\emph on
applyFnToGenes
\emph default
 function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\emph on
applyFnToGenes
\emph default
 function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
applyFnToGenes
\emph default
 is the main interface to Mega2R.
 You specify a list of genes to extract the transcripts for; then Mega2
 computes the list of markers that lie in each transcript.
 It computes an array of genotypes for each transcript from its markers
 and then it invokes a call back function that is supplied as an argument
 to the 
\emph on
applyFntoGenes
\emph default
 function.
 The call back function will be called with three arguments: the first is
 a matrix of genotypes for all the markers in the transcript, the second
 is a data frame of the markers for the transcript, and the third is a single
 row from an internal data frame that defines the transcript (minimally
 it has the at least names, ID, start position, end position, and chromosome).
 The call back function can apply whatever analysis is desired to the genotype
 matrix.
 It can also access the other data frames available in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

R environment
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
applyFnToGenes
\emph default
 is intended to be the front end.
 By giving it named arguments, you can indicate the call back should be
 applied to each of several sets of markers in arbitrary ranges on chromosomes
 (ranges_arg =) .
 Or you can specify the entire chromosome to define the range of markers
 (chrs_arg =).
 And finally, you can just specify an arbitrary list of markers (markers_args
 =).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\emph on
setRanges
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
 function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are several reasons that the officially provided ranges for the gene
 transcripts may not be to your liking.
 Sometimes the transcripts for a gene appear to have the same start/end
 position as another transcript but with a different name.
 Some transcripts overlap others.
 Transcripts have one start and one end but do not account for non coding
 regions, promoters, etc.
 Perhaps you would prefer to specify names and for each name provide a start
 position, end position and chromosome.
 The 
\emph on
setRanges
\emph default
 function has two arguments: a range data frame that has at least the start
 position, end position and chromosome 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

observations
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and a selector vector of 3 integer that indicates which observation variable
 in the first data frame contains the chromosome, start position and end
 position.
 The range and selector data are used as the default for 
\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
 has a call back function of three arguments just like 
\emph on
applyFnToGenes
\emph default
.
 With no additional arguments, it will iterate through each row of the default
 range, compute the markers contained between the start and end, compute
 the genotype matrix, and then invoke the call back.
 Alternatively, 
\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
 may be given a range argument and a selector argument which will cause
 it to use the ranges supplied rather than the default.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\emph on
applyFnToMarkers
\emph default
 function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
applyFnToMarkers
\emph default
 is the simplest function that uses the previously discussed call back function.
 It takes one additional argument that is a data frame of markers following
 the column layout of the 
\series bold
marker
\series default
 data frame.
 It creates a genotype matrix for these markers and then invokes the call
 back function with the genotype matrix, the marker argument data frame
 and NULL.
 (NULL because there is no range information.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection

\emph on
mega2pedgene
\emph default
 Package Functions 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:mega2pedgene-Package-Functions"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The CRAN pedgene package, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Gene-Level Statistics for Pedigree Data
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/pedgene/index.html
\end_layout

\end_inset

, performs 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Gene-level association tests with disease status for pedigree data: kernel
 and burden association statistics
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 It would serve as an example for 
\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
 mechanism described earlier.
 (This is especially true since we have the results of running this test
 on many genes for a specific set of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Ranges
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 on one of our private test datasets.
 We got the same results as our previous manual run.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First call 
\emph on
init_pedgene
\emph default
 rather than 
\emph on
read.Mega2DB
\emph default
; the former uses a function in the 
\emph on
mega2
\emph default
 package to load a Mega2 database (
\emph on
dbmega2_import
\emph default
).
 Then it generates a modified the 
\series bold
fam
\series default
 data frame it must eliminate samples where the case/control is not known.
 In addition, 
\emph on
init_pedgene 
\emph default
computes and saves some state into the environment that it will use later.
 
\emph on
run_pedgene
\emph default
 invokes 
\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
 on the default ranges.
 The call back function, 
\emph on
DOpedgene
\emph default
, replicates the code that we used in our previous study.
 The CRAN package 
\emph on
Pedgene
\emph default
 requires the count of the minor allele for each genotypes; DOpedgene does
 this transformation of its input genotype data.
 Then it calls the 
\emph on
pedgene
\emph default
 function with several different marker weights and returns the p-value
 of the kernel and burden association statistics for each weight.
 Note: The results for our private data are the same, but the original test
 code harness was hand coded and hard to change.
\end_layout

