\begin_layout Standard
All this information is stored in a Mega2 database.
 The code in the provided functions illustrates how to assemble the correspondin
g data from the Mega2 data frames.
 The 
\emph on
mkVCFphe
\emph default
 is a bit subtle.
 The database contains a raw vector of 8 bytes * the number of phenotypes
 (per person).
 The 8 bytes are either one double precision float number or two integers
 depending on the encoding in the 
\series bold
locus_table
\series default
 (quantitative or affection status).
 
\emph on
readBin
\emph default
 is used for converting the raw bits in each reach 8 byte vector to the
 correct form.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One interesting aspect of the code design is how we write data for a large
 numbers of markers without needing large amount of memory.
 We build the output a chunk at a time, where a chunk contains 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

X
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 markers (currently 1000).
 We generate the genotype matrix and get related data for each chunk then
 append the results for these markers to the .vcf file and other files.
 Then, we get a new chunk and repeat.
 This looping strategy might prove useful for other situations where the
 data can be generated incrementally.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection

\emph on
mega2genabel
\emph default
 Package Functions
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:mega2genabel-Package-Functions"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The GenABEL CRAN package provides may functions for GWAS analysis.
 It also accepts GWAS data in several convenient formats.
 But Mega2 accepts input in still more formats, notably, PLINK bed, VCF,
 IMPUTE2 and even linkage.
 Thus 
\emph on
Mega2GenABEL
\emph default
 can be a bridge to easily convert your data for GenABEL analysis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You must call the 
\emph on
read.Mega2DB 
\emph default
function from the 
\emph on
mega2
\emph default
 package and should save the returned environment into ENV.
 The function, 
\emph on
Mega2GenABEL
\emph default
, returns a gwaa.data-class object.
 It operates by writing the Mega2 data into one of the formats that GenABEL
 supports; currently, this is PLINK .tped format.
 This requires a .fam file, a .phe file and a .tped file.
 The latter looks very much like a VCF .vcf file with entries in each row
 for the genotypes of each marker.
 (There are less fields before the genotype data in a .tped file and the
 genotype data specify real nucleotides rather than VCF's REF/ALT field
 references.) 
\emph on
Mega2GenABEL
\emph default
 calls the GenABEL 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

glue
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 functions to process the a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

.tped
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 file into a generic GenABEL 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

raw
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 format file (
\emph on
convert.snp.tped
\emph default
) and then it converts that raw format and a phenotypefile to a GenABEL
 gwaa.data-class object via 
\emph on
load.gwaa.data
\emph default
.
\end_layout

